GitHub sign-in as Triage, not Visual Studio Code (ROADMAP P0-8, DV-15).

VS Code's open-source GitHub sign-in uses VS Code's own GitHub OAuth app
(client ID 01ab8ac9400c4e429b23), so GitHub told users they were authorizing
"Visual Studio Code". Triage uses the OAuth app the owner registered for it
(device flow on, token expiry off, no client secret). Microsoft adds VS Code's
client secret only to its own builds, so Triage signs in with the device flow
either way.

diff --git a/extensions/github-authentication/src/config.ts b/extensions/github-authentication/src/config.ts
index 8cd3a9ed79310ae6ee1bd54f47d283e7b9cdc354..7a2780a6016bc409d2e050fe41a79c99054c906b 100644
--- a/extensions/github-authentication/src/config.ts
+++ b/extensions/github-authentication/src/config.ts
@@ -14,6 +14,10 @@ export interface IConfig {
 // NOTE: GitHub client secrets cannot be secured when running in a native client so in other words, the client secret is
 // not really a secret... so we allow the client secret in code. It is brought in before we publish VS Code. Reference:
 // https://docs.github.com/en/apps/oauth-apps/building-oauth-apps/best-practices-for-creating-an-oauth-app#client-secrets
+// Triage (ROADMAP P0-8): Triage's own GitHub OAuth app, registered by the owner
+// with the device flow on and no client secret, so GitHub names Triage, not
+// Visual Studio Code, when a user signs in. Without a secret, sign-in uses the
+// device flow (see getFlows in flows.ts).
 export const Config: IConfig = {
-	gitHubClientId: '01ab8ac9400c4e429b23'
+	gitHubClientId: 'Ov23liajKvj7l41nmMfw'
 };
